---- DATA STRUCTURES ----

>> A1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

The following declarations are added in devices/timer.c:

struct sleep_waiter
  {
    uint64_t wake_tick;
    struct semaphore wake;
    struct list_elem elem;
  };

Purpose of struct sleep_waiter: One pending sleep, stored on its caller's
stack until the timer signals completion.

Purpose of wake_tick: Absolute earliest wakeup tick; unsigned addition
avoids signed deadline overflow for a large positive sleep duration.

Purpose of wake: A semaphore initialized to zero, used to block the
caller and retain a wakeup delivered before it blocks.

Purpose of elem: Links this pending sleep into the ordered sleepers list.

static struct list sleepers;

Purpose of sleepers: Pending sleeps ordered by increasing wake_tick,
shared by timer_sleep() and the timer interrupt handler.

No fields are added to struct thread.  Each timer_sleep() call owns its
local waiter, so no heap allocation or permanent per-thread alarm data
is required.  The existing global ticks declaration is unchanged.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

timer_sleep() requires interrupts enabled and a caller outside interrupt
context.  It returns immediately for a zero or negative duration.
Otherwise, it initializes a stack-local waiter's semaphore to zero.

It briefly disables interrupts, reads ticks, calculates the absolute
deadline as (uint64_t) ticks + (uint64_t) sleep_ticks, and inserts the
waiter into sleepers in ascending deadline order.  The strict comparison
preserves insertion order among equal deadlines.  It then restores the
previous interrupt level and calls sema_down().  The thread blocks if
the timer has not already signaled its semaphore.  It does not poll the
clock or repeatedly call thread_yield().

On each timer interrupt, the handler increments ticks and calls the
existing thread_tick().  It then examines the front of sleepers.  While
the front deadline is at or before the current tick, it removes that
record and calls sema_up() on its semaphore.  If a thread is blocked on
that semaphore, it becomes ready; actual execution depends on normal
scheduling.  The handler stops when the list is empty or its earliest
remaining deadline is in the future.  All sleeps due at that tick are
signaled, including simultaneous deadlines.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The queue is kept sorted when a thread registers its sleep, so the
interrupt handler does not scan sleepers whose deadlines are later.
It does no allocation, sorting, or blocking.  Removing the front list
element is constant time.  Each private semaphore has at most one
waiting thread.

The added alarm work per interrupt is O(k + 1), where k is the number
of due sleepers removed during that interrupt.  An interrupt with no
due sleepers performs only O(1) alarm work.  Insertion costs O(n) in
thread context for n queued sleepers; this is a deliberate tradeoff to
keep the frequently executed interrupt path short.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

Each caller initializes its own waiter and semaphore before publishing
the waiter to the shared queue.  In this uniprocessor Pintos kernel,
disabling interrupts around reading ticks and inserting into sleepers
prevents both a timer interrupt and timer-driven scheduling from
interleaving those shared-state operations.  The code does not yield or
block inside that critical section.

After restoring interrupts, the caller waits using sema_down().  The
semaphore implements blocking and wakeup synchronization; interrupt
disabling is not used to make the caller wait for time to pass.  The
short interrupt-disabled section specifically protects data also used
by the interrupt handler, which cannot acquire a sleeping lock.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

The semaphore is initialized before the waiter is added to sleepers.
Deadline calculation and insertion occur with interrupts disabled, so
the handler cannot observe a partially initialized or partially linked
record, and ticks cannot change between the snapshot and insertion.

An interrupt can occur after interrupts are restored but before the
caller reaches sema_down().  This does not lose the wakeup: sema_up()
increments the semaphore's value, and the later sema_down() consumes
that value without blocking.  If the caller reaches sema_down() first,
the existing semaphore implementation atomically checks its value and
blocks the caller; a later sema_up() unblocks it.

The handler removes the waiter from sleepers before signaling it.
The stack-local record remains valid while timer_sleep() is executing,
including while the caller is blocked.  When the caller can finally
return and release its stack storage, its record is no longer linked
into the shared queue.  The handler does not access the record after
signaling it.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

The ordered queue and per-sleep semaphore provide blocking without busy
waiting, a simple lifetime rule, and protection against early signals.
They reuse Pintos list and synchronization primitives and do not expand
struct thread or require heap allocation.  Unsigned deadline addition
also avoids undefined signed arithmetic when a positive duration is
very large.

An alternative is an unordered sleep list scanned on every timer tick.
It makes insertion O(1), but pays O(n) interrupt work even when no sleep
is due.  The selected design pays O(n) when registering a sleep and
O(k + 1) during an interrupt, which is preferable for an interrupt that
runs regularly while most deadlines are still in the future.

The tradeoffs are one small waiter on each sleeping caller's limited
kernel stack and an O(n) insertion performed with interrupts disabled.
A large number of sleepers can therefore increase registration latency.
For this project, the simple list avoids the extra code and maintenance
of a heap while keeping the interrupt path small.  A heap could reduce
insertion cost if the workload required much larger sleep queues.

